{"skill":{"slug":"pr-reviewer","displayName":"pr-reviewer"},"version":{"version":"1.0.1","createdAt":1771529167274,"changelog":"Fix security scan flags: declare runtime dependencies, document env vars and write paths","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":3544,"sha256":"fa8ba8cfeb103ecc317eaba66d51e44f91ec2398c2c16448b8818a121aa12457","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/pr-review.sh","size":18797,"sha256":"1587252d1bfcf4544c8a80bbbc9771c7f109908fb8ae64468d47e9a66b75c256","contentType":"application/x-sh"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2269,"sha256":"038d060d7d55fd7603a477a633fee3a3e5c4d9308ef9ccd56195b8069a32c6b6","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789029568443,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"776e525d92874c89fe747c0526593c3ecf11db17338d43c8a9bdd129dc445972","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/776e525d92874c89fe747c0526593c3ecf11db17338d43c8a9bdd129dc445972","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: pr-reviewer\nVersion: 1.0.1\n\nThe OpenClaw skill `pr-reviewer` is designed for automated GitHub PR code review, utilizing `gh` CLI and Python. It is classified as suspicious due to critical vulnerabilities rather than malicious intent. Specifically, the `scripts/pr-review.sh` script allows arbitrary file writes if the `PR_REVIEW_STATE` or `PR_REVIEW_OUTDIR` environment variables are set to sensitive paths (e.g., `/etc/passwd`), as there is no path sanitization or restriction. Additionally, there is a potential for shell injection in the `run_local_lint` function, where filenames obtained from GitHub PRs are directly expanded into `ruff` and `golangci-lint` commands, which could lead to arbitrary command execution if a malicious actor crafts a PR with specially named files containing shell metacharacters. No evidence of intentional data exfiltration, persistence, or obfuscation was found.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160965096},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":17,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":4,"checkedAt":1789029568443},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"The skill mostly matches its PR-review purpose, but its review script has a serious unsafe-code path where a crafted pull-request filename can run local Python code during review.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Using gh, python3, and optional linters is proportionate for PR review, but the script processes attacker-controlled GitHub PR metadata with local interpreter privileges and an authenticated gh environment.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"No installer, remote payload retrieval, dependency installation, or tool wrapping was found; metadata only declares required binaries, though it does not define a tighter tool or write scope.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The skill discloses GitHub authentication, local state/report files, and a post command, but remote commenting and cron-style use would benefit from stronger confirmation guidance; the documented quick-start path also differs from the packaged script path.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"Local state and markdown reports are expected and disclosed, and there is no system-level persistence; however, the post command uses the user's authenticated gh session to publish the generated report to GitHub, and the unsafe filename handling could expose that same environment to attacker-controlled code.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Automated PR review, GitHub reads, local report generation, linting, and optional PR commenting are coherent with the stated purpose, but untrusted PR filenames are interpolated into a python3 -c program during test-coverage checks, creating local code-execution risk while reviewing a PR.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Review this skill carefully before installing or running it on untrusted pull requests. Use a low-privilege GitHub account or token, avoid running it in an environment with unrelated secrets, and do not enable cron or automated review until the filename-to-Python injection issue is fixed by passing PR file data through stdin, argv, or JSON rather than embedding it in source code.","findings":"[LP3] expected: Local state and report writes are documented and purpose-aligned, so this is not suspicious by itself, but narrower permission disclosure would make the authority clearer.\n[SQP-2] expected: Posting a PR comment is an expected feature and is exposed as a separate post command, but users should treat it as a remote write action that uses their authenticated GitHub identity.\n[SQP-2] expected: Creating the review directory and state file is consistent with smart re-review behavior and is documented in SKILL.md.\n[SQP-2] expected: Uploading the saved report with gh pr comment is purpose-aligned for the post workflow, but a pre-post confirmation or clearer runtime notice would reduce accidental publication risk.\n[T09] unexpected: The static finding is supported by the script: get_pr_files reads PR-controlled paths and check_test_coverage embeds that string directly inside Python source, so crafted filenames can break out of the intended data string.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789029568443}}}}}